{"ptb_xl__comes_with__a_variety_of_labels_and_further_metadata": "The phrase 'comes with' indicates that PTB-XL is accompanied by or includes certain features or additional information as part of its offering. This suggests that users of PTB-XL can expect to find these features or information included with the dataset."},
{"a_variety_of_labels_and_further_metadata": "A variety of labels and further metadata refers to the additional information and categorizations that are provided alongside the core data in PTB-XL. These labels and metadata can include diagnostic statements, patient demographics, and other relevant data points that enhance the utility of the dataset for research and analysis."},
{"the_presented_experiments": "The presented experiments refer to the series of tests and analyses conducted using the PTB-XL dataset, aimed at evaluating and illustrating various analytical capabilities."},
{"the_presented_experiments__serve__two_purposes": "The predicate \"serve\" indicates that the experiments have been designed and executed to fulfill specific goals or functions within the context of the study."},
{"two_purposes": "The two purposes of the experiments are to provide initial benchmarking results for future reference and to demonstrate the versatility of analyses that can be performed using the PTB-XL dataset."},
{"the_first_purpose": "The first purpose refers to one of the two main objectives of the experiments presented in the context. It is specifically related to establishing a baseline for future evaluations."},
{"the_first_purpose__is__to_provide_first_benchmarking_results_for_future_reference": "The predicate 'is' serves as a linking verb that connects the subject 'The first purpose' with its intended outcome or goal, which is described in the object of the triple."},
{"to_provide_first_benchmarking_results_for_future_reference": "To provide first benchmarking results for future reference means to establish initial performance metrics or standards that can be used as a comparison point in subsequent studies or experiments."},
{"the_second_purpose": "The second purpose refers to one of the two main goals of the experiments presented in the context. It is specifically focused on demonstrating the wide range of analyses possible."},
{"the_second_purpose__is__to_illustrate_the_versatility_of_analyses_that_can_be_carried_out_based_on_the_ptb_xl_dataset": "The predicate 'is' serves as a linking verb that connects the subject, which is the second purpose, to its description or function, which is to illustrate the versatility of analyses."},
{"to_illustrate_the_versatility_of_analyses_that_can_be_carried_out_based_on_the_ptb_xl_dataset": "The object, 'to illustrate the versatility of analyses that can be carried out based on the PTB-XL dataset,' describes the specific aim of the second purpose. It highlights the potential for various types of analyses using the PTB-XL dataset."},
{"section_iii_b": "Section III-B is a part of a document or study that focuses on the evaluation of certain methodologies or models. It is specifically designed to assess the performance and effectiveness of classifiers under various conditions related to ECG statements.|Here is another description: Section III-B is a part of a document or study that focuses on evaluating classifiers for various selections and granularities of ECG statements. It is a critical segment that provides insights into the performance and capabilities of different classifiers within the context of ECG analysis.|Here is another description: Section III-B is a part of a document or study where specific model architectures are detailed. This section serves as the reference point from which the model architectures used in the current experiment are applied, with modifications to their final layers to accommodate the new tasks of sex and age prediction."},
{"section_iii_b__evaluates__classifiers_for_different_selections_and_granularities_of_ecg_statements": "The predicate 'evaluates' indicates an action where Section III-B performs a critical assessment or analysis. This involves testing and measuring the performance, accuracy, and reliability of the classifiers mentioned in the context."},
{"classifiers_for_different_selections_and_granularities_of_ecg_statements": "The object 'classifiers for different selections and granularities of ECG statements' refers to a set of analytical models or algorithms that are designed to categorize or classify Electrocardiogram (ECG) data. These classifiers are tested under various conditions, including different selections and levels of detail (granularities) of ECG data, to assess their effectiveness and adaptability."},
{"section_iii_b__represents__the_core_of_the_analysis": "The predicate 'represents' in this context signifies that Section III-B is the embodiment or the primary example of the core analysis being discussed. It indicates that the content of Section III-B is central to understanding the main focus of the study or document."},
{"the_core_of_the_analysis": "The core of the analysis refers to the fundamental or most important part of the study or document's examination. In this case, it is the evaluation of classifiers for different selections and granularities of ECG statements, which is the central theme of Section III-B."},
{"section_iii_c": "Section III-C is a part of the document that focuses on validating the findings and investigating transfer learning aspects using the PTB-XL dataset for pretraining.|Here is another description: Section III-C is a part of a document or study that follows Section III-B and precedes Section III-D. It focuses on validating findings and exploring specific methodologies within the context of the PTB-XL dataset."},
{"section_iii_c__validates__findings_on_the_icbeb2018_dataset": "The predicate 'validates' in this context refers to the process of confirming the accuracy and reliability of the findings obtained from the experiments conducted in Section III-B by applying them to a different dataset."},
{"findings_on_the_icbeb2018_dataset": "The findings on the ICBEB2018 dataset are the results and insights gained from applying the classifiers and analyses developed using the PTB-XL dataset to the ICBEB2018 dataset, serving as a validation step to ensure the robustness of the findings."},
{"section_iii_c__investigates__aspects_of_transfer_learning_using_ptb_xl_for_pretraining": "The predicate 'investigates' signifies a thorough examination or study of a particular topic or methodology. In this context, it refers to the detailed exploration of transfer learning techniques using the PTB-XL dataset for pretraining purposes."},
{"aspects_of_transfer_learning_using_ptb_xl_for_pretraining": "Aspects of transfer learning using PTB-XL for pretraining refer to the various elements or components of transfer learning that are explored in the study. This includes the application of the PTB-XL dataset, a comprehensive ECG database, as a pretraining resource to enhance the performance of machine learning models in related tasks."},
{"section_iii_d": "Section III-D is a part of a document or report that focuses on specific experiments and analyses related to the PTB-XL dataset. It follows sections III-B and III-C, which deal with evaluating classifiers and investigating transfer learning, respectively."},
{"section_iii_d__illustrates__ways_of_leveraging_further_metadata_within_ptb_xl_to_construct_age_and_sex_prediction_models": "The predicate 'illustrates' in this context means that Section III-D provides examples or demonstrations. It shows how certain methods or techniques can be applied using the PTB-XL dataset."},
{"ways_of_leveraging_further_metadata_within_ptb_xl_to_construct_age_and_sex_prediction_models": "The object 'ways of leveraging further metadata within PTB-XL to construct age and sex prediction models' refers to the specific methods or techniques discussed in Section III-D. These methods involve using additional information (metadata) available in the PTB-XL dataset to create models that can predict a person's age and sex."},
{"primary_performance_metric": "The primary performance metric is the main measure used to evaluate the effectiveness of classification experiments. It provides a comprehensive assessment of how well the classification algorithm performs across different classes."},
{"primary_performance_metric__is__macro_averaged_area_under_the_receiver_operating_characteristic_curve_auc": "The predicate \"is\" signifies that the primary performance metric is specifically identified as the macro-averaged area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC). This identification highlights the chosen method for evaluating classification performance."},
{"macro_averaged_area_under_the_receiver_operating_characteristic_curve_auc": "The macro-averaged area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) is a performance metric that averages the area under the curve for each class. This approach is particularly useful in scenarios with class imbalance, as it ensures that each class contributes equally to the overall performance assessment."},
{"auc": "AUC, or Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve, is a performance metric used in classification experiments to evaluate the discriminative power of a classification algorithm. It is particularly useful in scenarios where class imbalance is expected and where a more complete picture of the algorithm's performance is desired without the influence of threshold optimization.|Here is another description: AUC, or Area Under the Curve, is a performance metric used in the evaluation of binary classification models. It represents the probability that a model ranks a random positive example more highly than a random negative example."},
{"auc__is_obtained_by__averaging_class_wise_aucs_over_all_classes": "The phrase 'is obtained by' indicates the method or process through which the subject (AUC) is calculated or derived. In this context, it specifies that the AUC value is the result of a specific averaging process applied to class-wise AUCs."},
{"averaging_class_wise_aucs_over_all_classes": "Averaging class-wise AUCs over all classes refers to the process of calculating the AUC for each class individually and then taking the average of these values. This method, known as macro-averaging, is preferred in scenarios where class imbalance is present, as it gives equal weight to each class regardless of its size."},
{"metrics": "Metrics in this context refer to performance indicators used to evaluate the effectiveness of classification algorithms. These metrics are crucial for assessing how well a classifier distinguishes between different classes in a dataset."},
{"metrics__can_be_evaluated_based_on__soft_classifier_outputs": "The phrase \"can be evaluated based on\" indicates a dependency relationship where the effectiveness or performance of the subject (metrics) is determined or assessed using the object (soft classifier outputs). This relationship suggests that the metrics are contingent upon the outputs generated by the classifier before any thresholding is applied."},
{"soft_classifier_outputs": "Soft classifier outputs refer to the raw, continuous output values produced by a classification algorithm, which indicate the likelihood of each class for a given input. These outputs have not yet been thresholded to make discrete class predictions, allowing for a more nuanced evaluation of the classifier's performance."},
{"thresholding": "Thresholding in the context of classification algorithms refers to the process of setting a boundary or cut-off value to convert continuous output scores into binary decisions. It is a critical step in many classification tasks to determine whether a given input belongs to a particular class or not."},
{"thresholding__has_not_been_applied_yet__to_get_a_more_complete_picture_of_the_discriminative_power_of_a_given_classification_algorithm": "The phrase \"has not been applied yet\" indicates that the process of thresholding has not been implemented or executed at this stage. This suggests that the evaluation or analysis is being conducted on raw, unthresholded outputs from the classification algorithm."},
{"to_get_a_more_complete_picture_of_the_discriminative_power_of_a_given_classification_algorithm": "The goal of obtaining \"a more complete picture of the discriminative power of a given classification algorithm\" refers to the desire to assess the algorithm's ability to distinguish between different classes without the influence of thresholding. This approach allows for a comprehensive evaluation of the algorithm's performance across a range of possible thresholds, providing insights into its overall effectiveness."},
{"macro_averaging": "Macro-averaging is a method used in performance evaluation of classification algorithms, where it calculates the average of a metric across all classes, giving equal weight to each class regardless of the number of instances."},
{"macro_averaging__is_preferred_in__our_setting": "The preference for macro-averaging in this context indicates a deliberate choice of evaluation metric due to specific characteristics of the classification task, such as expected class imbalance."},
{"our_setting": "Our setting refers to the specific conditions or environment in which the classification experiments are conducted, characterized by the need to evaluate classifier performance without the influence of class imbalance and without premature thresholding.|Here is another description: \"Our setting\" refers to the specific environment or conditions under which the classification experiments are conducted. This setting involves the use of macro-averaged AUC as the primary performance metric, focusing on soft classifier outputs without thresholding, and preferring macro-averaging due to the expected class imbalance."},
{"class_imbalance": "Class imbalance refers to a situation in machine learning where the distribution of classes in a dataset is uneven, meaning some classes have significantly more instances than others. This imbalance can affect the performance of classification algorithms, as they may become biased towards the majority class."},
{"class_imbalance__is_expected_in__our_setting": "The phrase \"is expected in\" indicates a prediction or assumption about the presence of a certain condition within a specified context. In this case, it suggests that class imbalance is anticipated to be a characteristic of the described setting."},
{"score": "The score refers to a metric or evaluation measure used in the context of multi-label classification problems, particularly focusing on how well the classification aligns with the dataset's characteristics."},
{"score__to_be_dominated_by__a_few_large_classes": "To be dominated by indicates a significant influence or prevalence of certain elements over others within the context of the score, suggesting that the score is heavily swayed by a specific subset of the data."},
{"a_few_large_classes": "A few large classes represent a small number of categories within the dataset that contain a disproportionately large amount of data or influence, impacting the overall score and its interpretation."},
{"distribution_of_pathologies": "The distribution of pathologies refers to how different medical conditions or diseases are represented within a specific dataset. This distribution can vary based on the dataset's origin and the method of data collection.|Here is another description: The distribution of pathologies refers to how different medical conditions or diseases are represented within a dataset. In this context, it highlights the uneven representation of various pathologies, which does not align with their natural prevalence in the general population."},
{"distribution_of_pathologies__does_not_follow__the_natural_distribution_in_the_population": "The phrase \"does not follow\" indicates a discrepancy or deviation between the expected or natural pattern and the observed pattern in the dataset. In this context, it highlights that the distribution of pathologies in the dataset is not aligned with what would be naturally expected in the general population."},
{"the_natural_distribution_in_the_population": "The natural distribution in the population refers to the expected or typical way that pathologies or diseases are distributed among people in the general population. This distribution is influenced by various factors such as genetics, environment, and lifestyle."},
{"distribution_of_pathologies__reflects__the_data_collection_process": "The predicate \"reflects\" indicates that the distribution of pathologies is a direct consequence or representation of another factor. Here, it suggests that the way pathologies are distributed in the dataset is influenced by external processes rather than natural occurrence."},
{"the_data_collection_process": "The data collection process refers to the methods and procedures used to gather and compile the dataset. In this context, it implies that the way data was collected has significantly influenced the distribution of pathologies within the dataset, leading to an unnatural representation of diseases."},
{"metrics_for_multi_label_classification_problems": "Metrics for multi-label classification problems refer to the various methods and techniques used to evaluate the performance of models that classify instances into multiple labels simultaneously. These metrics are crucial for assessing how well a model can handle complex classification tasks where an instance can belong to more than one class."},
{"metrics_for_multi_label_classification_problems__are__a_wide_field": "The predicate \"are\" signifies the state or condition of the subject, indicating that metrics for multi-label classification problems exist and are recognized as a significant area of study within the field of machine learning and data science."},
{"a_wide_field": "A wide field implies that the area of metrics for multi-label classification problems is extensive, encompassing a broad range of approaches, techniques, and research. This field includes various types of metrics, such as sample-centric and label-centric metrics, and is continually evolving with new developments and reviews, as indicated by references like [47] and [48]."},
{"review_on_multi_label_classification_metrics_and_algorithms": "A comprehensive review that delves into the various metrics and algorithms used for multi-label classification tasks, providing insights into their applications and effectiveness."},
{"review_on_multi_label_classification_metrics_and_algorithms__see__47": "The predicate'see' directs the reader to reference a specific source, in this case, [47], for more detailed information on the subject of multi-label classification metrics and algorithms."},
{"47": "Reference [47] is a cited work that offers a detailed review on the metrics and algorithms specific to multi-label classification, serving as a valuable resource for those looking to deepen their understanding of the topic."},
{"one": "The subject 'one' refers to an unspecified entity or general perspective within the context of discussing multi-label classification metrics. It is used to introduce a distinction or categorization in the field.|Here is another description: The object \"one\" in this context represents the total sum of probabilities that the output probabilities are constrained to. This ensures that the probabilities across all possible diagnostic outcomes add up to one, adhering to the fundamental rule of probability theory that the sum of probabilities for all possible outcomes must equal one."},
{"one__distinguishes__sample_centric_and_label_centric_metrics": "The predicate 'distinguishes' indicates the action of making a clear differentiation or separation between two or more categories or concepts. In this context, it is used to highlight the distinction between different types of metrics used in multi-label classification."},
{"sample_centric_and_label_centric_metrics": "The object 'sample-centric and label-centric metrics' refers to two categories of metrics used in multi-label classification problems. Sample-centric metrics evaluate performance based on individual samples, while label-centric metrics assess performance based on individual labels or classes."},
{"macro_auc": "Macro AUC is a statistical measure used in multi-label classification problems to evaluate the performance of a model. It calculates the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve for each label and then takes the average, providing an overall performance metric that is not biased towards any particular class.|Here is another description: Macro AUC is an evaluation metric that calculates the Area Under the Curve (AUC) for each class in a multi-class classification problem and then takes the average. It is used to measure the performance of algorithms, providing a balanced assessment across all classes, especially in datasets with class imbalances."},
{"macro_auc__is__an_example_of_label_centric_metrics": "The term 'is' in this context signifies that macro AUC falls under the category of label-centric metrics. This means that the metric focuses on the performance across different labels or classes rather than individual samples."},
{"an_example_of_label_centric_metrics": "Label-centric metrics are performance evaluation metrics in multi-label classification that emphasize the distribution and performance across different labels. Unlike sample-centric metrics, which focus on individual data points, label-centric metrics like macro AUC provide insights into how well a model performs across all classes, making them crucial for datasets with imbalanced class distributions."},
{"sample_centric_metrics": "Sample-centric metrics are a type of evaluation metric used in multi-label classification problems that focus on the performance of the model at the level of individual samples or instances. They provide insights into how well the model predicts the labels for each sample in the dataset."},
{"sample_centric_metrics__see__e_g_48": "The predicate \"see\" in this context directs the reader to reference additional information or examples that elaborate on the subject. It suggests that further details or explanations about sample-centric metrics can be found in the cited source."},
{"e_g_48": "The object \"e.g. [48]\" refers to a specific citation or reference within the context. It indicates that example or further discussion of sample-centric metrics can be found in the work cited as [48], which likely provides more detailed information or examples related to these metrics."},
{"results_for_sample_centric_metrics": "The results for sample-centric metrics refer to the evaluation metrics that focus on individual samples or instances within a dataset, as opposed to label-centric metrics which aggregate performance across all labels."},
{"results_for_sample_centric_metrics__are_provided_in__the_code_repository": "The phrase \"are provided in\" indicates that the results or data mentioned are made available or can be found within a specified location or resource."},
{"the_code_repository": "The code repository is a storage location where the source code, along with other project-related files such as documentation, configuration files, and possibly the results of experiments or analyses, are stored and managed. In this context, it is where the results for sample-centric metrics can be accessed."},
{"icbeb2018": "ICBEB2018 is a dataset that has been utilized for various performance evaluations in machine learning and signal processing tasks. It is specifically noted for its use in benchmarking classification algorithms.|Here is another description: ICBEB2018 is a dataset that was selected as the training dataset for the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020. It is used for evaluating performance metrics in machine learning classification tasks, particularly in the context of biomedical data.|Here is another description: ICBEB2018 is a dataset used for training and validating classifiers. It serves as a benchmark for evaluating the performance of various machine learning models in terms of their ability to classify data accurately."},
{"icbeb2018__was_selected_as__training_dataset_for_the_physionet_cinc_challenge_2020": "The phrase \"was selected as\" indicates that ICBEB2018 was chosen or designated for a particular role or purpose. In this context, it highlights the dataset's adoption for a significant and specific application."},
{"training_dataset_for_the_physionet_cinc_challenge_2020": "The PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020 is an annual competition focused on computational physiology, where participants develop algorithms to solve problems related to physiological signal processing. The training dataset is crucial for developing and testing these algorithms."},
{"icbeb2018__reports__two_further_performance_metrics": "The predicate \"reports\" indicates the action of providing or presenting specific information or metrics. In this context, it refers to the act of documenting and sharing additional performance metrics for the ICBEB2018 dataset."},
{"two_further_performance_metrics": "The two further performance metrics reported for ICBEB2018 are a macro-averaged F\u03b2-score (with \u03b2=2) and a macro-averaged G\u03b2-score (with \u03b2=2). These metrics are used to evaluate the performance of classification algorithms, with the F\u03b2-score balancing precision and recall, and the G\u03b2-score providing a different balance by incorporating false positives and false negatives in its calculation.|Here is another description: Two additional performance metrics were reported for the ICBEB2018 dataset, which was used as the training dataset for the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020. These metrics were specifically chosen for comparability reasons."},
{"two_further_performance_metrics__were_used_as__evaluation_metrics_during_the_first_stage_of_the_challenge": "The predicate 'were used as' indicates that the two performance metrics served a specific role or function within the context of the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020. They were employed to evaluate the performance of the models during the initial stage of the challenge."},
{"evaluation_metrics_during_the_first_stage_of_the_challenge": "The evaluation metrics during the first stage of the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020 included a macro-averaged F\u03b2-score (with \u03b2=2) and a macro-averaged G\u03b2-score (with \u03b2=2). These metrics were used to assess the performance of the models, with sample weights chosen inversely proportional to the number of labels."},
{"macro_averaged_f\u03b2_score": "The macro-averaged F\u03b2-score is a performance metric used to evaluate the effectiveness of a classification algorithm, particularly in scenarios where recall is more important than precision. It is calculated by averaging the F\u03b2-scores across all classes, with each class weighted equally."},
{"macro_averaged_f\u03b2_score__has__\u03b2_2": "The predicate 'has' in this context indicates that the macro-averaged F\u03b2-score is characterized by a specific value of \u03b2, which influences the balance between recall and precision in the evaluation."},
{"\u03b2_2": "The value \u03b2 = 2 signifies that the F\u03b2-score places more emphasis on recall than on precision. This is because a higher \u03b2 value increases the weight given to recall in the harmonic mean calculation of the F\u03b2-score.|Here is another description: The object \u03b2 = 2 represents the specific value assigned to the parameter \u03b2 in the calculation of the macro-averaged G\u03b2-score. This value of \u03b2 = 2 assigns more weight to recall than precision, which can be a desirable property in certain classification tasks, though it may be questionable for classes where high precision is required."},
{"macro_averaged_g\u03b2_score": "The macro-averaged G\u03b2-score is a performance metric used in evaluating classification algorithms, specifically in the context of the ICBEB2018 dataset and the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020. It is calculated as the average G\u03b2-score across all classes, where G\u03b2 is defined as TP/(TP + FP + \u03b2\u00b7FN), with sample weights chosen inversely proportional to the number of labels."},
{"macro_averaged_g\u03b2_score__has__\u03b2_2": "The predicate 'has' in this context indicates that the macro-averaged G\u03b2-score is characterized by a specific value of \u03b2, which is set to 2 in this case. This value of \u03b2 influences the weighting between recall and precision in the G\u03b2-score calculation."},
{"g\u03b2": "G\u03b2 is a performance metric used in the evaluation of classification algorithms, specifically in the context of the ICBEB2018 dataset and the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020. It is a macro-averaged score that incorporates both precision and recall, with an adjustable parameter \u03b2 that allows for different weightings of these two metrics."},
{"g\u03b2__is_defined_as__tp_tp_fp_\u03b2_fn": "The predicate \"is defined as\" indicates that G\u03b2 is mathematically formulated as TP/(TP + FP + \u03b2 \u00b7 FN). This definition encapsulates the relationship between true positives, false positives, and false negatives, adjusted by the parameter \u03b2 to balance the importance of recall and precision in the evaluation metric."},
{"tp_tp_fp_\u03b2_fn": "TP/(TP + FP + \u03b2 \u00b7 FN) is the mathematical expression that defines the G\u03b2 score. It calculates the ratio of true positives to the sum of true positives, false positives, and a weighted count of false negatives, where the weight is determined by the parameter \u03b2. This expression is used to evaluate the performance of classification algorithms, particularly in scenarios where recall is more important than precision."},
{"sample_weights": "Sample weights refer to the values assigned to individual samples in a dataset to influence the learning process of a model. In the context of the ICBEB2018 dataset used for the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020, these weights are crucial for balancing the impact of samples during training."},
{"sample_weights__are_chosen__inversely_proportional_to_the_number_of_labels": "The predicate \"are chosen\" indicates the method or criteria used to determine the sample weights. In this scenario, the weights are selected based on a specific rule that relates to the number of labels associated with each sample."},
{"inversely_proportional_to_the_number_of_labels": "The object \"inversely proportional to the number of labels\" describes the specific criterion used for choosing the sample weights. This means that samples with fewer labels receive higher weights, while those with more labels receive lower weights, aiming to balance the influence of samples with varying numbers of labels during model training."},
{"\u03b2_1": "The subject, \u03b2 > 1, refers to a value of \u03b2 greater than one in the context of performance metrics like the macro-averaged F\u03b2-score and G\u03b2-score used in evaluating classification algorithms. This value influences the balance between recall and precision in these metrics."},
{"\u03b2_1__allows_to_assign_more_weight_to__recall_than_precision": "The predicate, \"allows to assign more weight to,\" describes the relationship where a specific value of \u03b2 (greater than 1) in performance metrics enables a greater emphasis or importance to be placed on one component (recall) over another (precision)."},
{"recall_than_precision": "The object, \"recall than precision,\" refers to the preference given to recall over precision in the performance metrics when \u03b2 is greater than 1. Recall measures the proportion of actual positives that are correctly identified, while precision measures the proportion of positive identifications that are actually correct."},
{"applying_\u03b2_1_to_the_norm_class": "Applying \u03b2 > 1 to the NORM-class refers to the process of adjusting the \u03b2 parameter in performance metrics like the F\u03b2-score and G\u03b2-score to be greater than 1, specifically for the NORM class in a classification task. This adjustment assigns more weight to recall over precision."},
{"applying_\u03b2_1_to_the_norm_class__seems__questionable": "The predicate \"seems\" indicates an evaluation or judgment about the appropriateness or effectiveness of applying \u03b2 > 1 to the NORM-class. It suggests that there is a degree of uncertainty or skepticism about this application."},
{"questionable": "The object \"questionable\" describes the nature of the judgment about applying \u03b2 > 1 to the NORM-class. It implies that such an application may not be appropriate or effective, raising doubts about its validity in this specific context."},
{"high_precision": "High precision refers to the accuracy of a classification model in correctly identifying instances of a particular class, minimizing false positives. In the context of medical data classification, achieving high precision is crucial for reliable diagnosis and decision-making."},
{"high_precision__is_required_in__the_norm_class": "The phrase \"is required in\" indicates a necessary condition or prerequisite for effective performance within a specific context or application. In this case, it highlights the importance of high precision for the proper functioning of the NORM-class classification."},
{"the_norm_class": "The NORM-class represents a category or group within a classification system, likely referring to normal or baseline conditions in a medical context. Accurate classification of the NORM-class is essential for distinguishing it from other classes and ensuring the overall reliability of the classification model."},
{"corresponding_scores": "The corresponding scores refer to the macro-averaged _F\u03b2_ -score and macro-averaged _G\u03b2_ -score used as performance metrics in the evaluation of classification algorithms."},
{"corresponding_scores__are_sensitive_to__the_chosen_classification_threshold": "The sensitivity of the corresponding scores to the chosen classification threshold indicates that the values of these scores can vary significantly depending on the threshold set for classification, which complicates the assessment of the algorithm's performance."},
{"the_chosen_classification_threshold": "The chosen classification threshold is the specific value set to determine whether a sample is classified as positive or negative, which directly influences the calculation of performance metrics like _F\u03b2_ -score and _G\u03b2_ -score."},
{"classification_threshold": "The classification threshold is a critical parameter in machine learning models that determines the boundary for classifying data points into different categories. It plays a significant role in balancing the trade-off between precision and recall."},
{"classification_threshold__is_determined_by__maximizing_the_f\u03b2_g\u03b2_score_on_the_training_set": "The determination of the classification threshold is a crucial step in the model evaluation process. It involves selecting the optimal threshold value that maximizes a specific performance metric, ensuring the model performs well on unseen data."},
{"maximizing_the_f\u03b2_g\u03b2_score_on_the_training_set": "Maximizing the F\u03b2/G\u03b2-score on the training set is a method used to determine the optimal classification threshold. This approach aims to find the threshold that yields the highest F\u03b2/G\u03b2-score, a metric that balances precision and recall with a customizable emphasis on recall through the \u03b2 parameter.|Here is another description: Maximizing the F\u03b2/G\u03b2-score on the training set refers to the process of adjusting the classification threshold to achieve the highest possible F\u03b2/G\u03b2-score during the training phase of a machine learning model. This score is a performance metric that balances precision and recall, with a parameter \u03b2 that allows for assigning more weight to recall than precision."},
{"maximizing_the_f\u03b2_g\u03b2_score_on_the_training_set__is__an_undesirable_aspect": "The phrase \"is\" serves as a linking verb that connects the subject, maximizing the F\u03b2/G\u03b2-score on the training set, to its characterization as an undesirable aspect. It indicates that the action or process described by the subject has a negative connotation or implication within the context of the discussion."},
{"an_undesirable_aspect": "An undesirable aspect in this context refers to a characteristic or feature of the process that is considered negative or problematic. Specifically, it highlights the entanglement of the discriminative performance of the classification algorithm with the process of threshold determination, which can lead to issues in evaluating the true performance of the model."},
{"undesirable_aspect": "The undesirable aspect refers to a negative characteristic or feature within a system or process that complicates or negatively impacts its performance or outcomes."},
