{"non_overlapping_confidence_intervals__signify__statistically_significant_differences_between_the_classifiers": "The term'signify' in this context means that the non-overlapping nature of these confidence intervals indicates or points to a specific conclusion about the classifiers."},
{"statistically_significant_differences_between_the_classifiers": "Statistically significant differences between the classifiers mean that the performance metrics of these classifiers are different enough that the difference is unlikely to have occurred by random chance, based on the calculated confidence intervals.|Here is another description: Statistically significant differences between classifiers refer to the meaningful distinctions in performance metrics that can be confidently attributed to the classifiers themselves, rather than to random variation or sampling error. These differences are typically determined through statistical tests that account for the variability in the data."},
{"overlapping_confidence_intervals": "Overlapping confidence intervals refer to the ranges within which the true value of a statistical measure is expected to fall, based on the sample data. In the context of evaluating classifiers, these intervals are used to assess the uncertainty and variability of performance metrics."},
