{"stratified_sampling_procedure": "The stratified sampling procedure is a method used to ensure that each subset of a dataset contains a proportionate representation of the different classes or categories present in the entire dataset. This technique is particularly useful in maintaining the integrity of statistical analyses by preventing bias that could arise from uneven class distribution across different subsets."},
{"stratified_sampling_procedure__underlies__the_fold_assignments_in_15": "The term \"underlies\" indicates that the stratified sampling procedure serves as the foundational method or principle that supports or is integral to the creation or functioning of the fold assignments. In this context, it means that the stratified sampling approach is essential in determining how data is divided into different folds or subsets."},
{"the_fold_assignments_in_15": "The fold assignments in [15] refer to the specific divisions or partitions of the dataset into different folds, as described in reference [15]. These assignments are crucial for processes like cross-validation, where the dataset is split into training and testing sets to evaluate the performance of machine learning models. The stratified sampling procedure ensures that each fold maintains the overall class distribution of the dataset."},
