{"electrocardiography_ecg": "Electrocardiography (ECG) is a medical diagnostic tool used to record the electrical activity of the heart over a period of time using electrodes placed on the skin. It is a standard procedure in cardiology to detect various heart conditions.|Here is another description: Electrocardiography (ECG) is a non-invasive medical test that records the electrical activity of the heart. It is commonly used as a first-line examination for diagnosing cardiovascular diseases."},
{"electrocardiography_ecg__is__a_very_common_non_invasive_diagnostic_procedure": "The predicate 'is' in this context signifies the nature or characteristic of Electrocardiography (ECG), describing it as a type of diagnostic procedure."},
{"a_very_common_non_invasive_diagnostic_procedure": "A very common, non-invasive diagnostic procedure refers to a medical test that is frequently used and does not require breaking the skin or entering the body to obtain results, making it safe and widely accepted for routine and emergency use."},
{"ecg_interpretation": "ECG interpretation refers to the process of analyzing and understanding the data obtained from an electrocardiogram, a common non-invasive diagnostic procedure used to assess the electrical activity of the heart.|Here is another description: ECG interpretation is the process of analyzing and understanding the results of an electrocardiogram, a test that records the electrical activity of the heart. It is a critical skill in diagnosing cardiovascular diseases and assessing a patient's cardiac condition."},
{"ecg_interpretation__is_increasingly_supported_by__algorithms": "The phrase \"is increasingly supported by\" indicates a growing reliance or enhancement in the field of ECG interpretation through the use of specific tools or methods, in this case, algorithms."},
{"algorithms": "Algorithms in the context of ECG interpretation are computational methods or sets of rules designed to perform automatic analysis and classification of ECG data, aiding in the diagnostic process.|Here is another description: Algorithms in this context refer to a variety of computational methods and models used for processing and analyzing raw ECG signals. These algorithms are designed to extract meaningful patterns and features from the data, which can then be used for classification or prediction tasks.|Here is another description: Algorithms refer to a set of rules or procedures designed to solve specific problems or perform particular tasks. In the context of machine learning and data analysis, algorithms are crucial for processing and interpreting data.|Here is another description: The subject refers to algorithms that have been evaluated or are being discussed in the context of their performance on the PTB-XL dataset."},
{"progress_in_automatic_ecg_analysis": "The progress in automatic ECG analysis refers to the advancements and developments in the use of algorithms to interpret electrocardiograms, a common non-invasive diagnostic procedure.|Here is another description: The progress in automatic ECG analysis refers to the advancements and developments in the use of algorithms to interpret electrocardiograms, a common non-invasive diagnostic procedure."},
{"progress_in_automatic_ecg_analysis__has_been_hampered_by__a_lack_of_appropriate_datasets_for_training": "The phrase \"has been hampered by\" indicates that the progress in automatic ECG analysis has faced significant obstacles or challenges."},
