{"full_source_code_trained_models_and_the_benchmarking_infrastructure": "The full source code, trained models, and benchmarking infrastructure refer to the complete set of resources necessary for replicating and evaluating the experiments and results presented in the study. This includes the actual code used to implement the algorithms, the models that have been trained on the data, and the tools and frameworks used to benchmark the performance of these models."},
{"full_source_code_trained_models_and_the_benchmarking_infrastructure__released__in_a_corresponding_code_repository": "The term \"released\" indicates that the subject entities\u2014full source code, trained models, and benchmarking infrastructure\u2014have been made publicly available. This action allows other researchers and developers to access, use, and build upon these resources, promoting transparency, reproducibility, and collaboration in the scientific and technical community."},
{"in_a_corresponding_code_repository": "A corresponding code repository is a designated online platform where the source code, trained models, and benchmarking infrastructure are stored and made accessible. This repository serves as a central location for others to download, review, and utilize the resources, ensuring that the work is reproducible and can be further developed by the community."},
{"transfer_learning_in_the_ecg_context": "Transfer learning in the ECG context refers to the application of knowledge gained from one ECG dataset to improve performance on another, potentially smaller, ECG dataset. This approach leverages pre-existing models trained on large, annotated datasets to enhance the accuracy and efficiency of ECG analysis in scenarios where data is scarce."},
{"transfer_learning_in_the_ecg_context__first_reliable_assessment_provided__demonstrating_the_promising_prospects_of_transfer_learning_from_ptb_xl_to_other_ecg_classification_datasets_in_the_small_dataset_regime": "The phrase \"first reliable assessment provided\" indicates that this is the initial thorough and dependable evaluation of the effectiveness and feasibility of transfer learning techniques specifically within the domain of ECG data analysis. This assessment is crucial for validating the approach and establishing its credibility in the field."},
{"demonstrating_the_promising_prospects_of_transfer_learning_from_ptb_xl_to_other_ecg_classification_datasets_in_the_small_dataset_regime": "Demonstrating the promising prospects of transfer learning from PTB-XL to other ECG classification datasets in the small dataset regime involves showcasing the potential benefits and effectiveness of applying transfer learning techniques. This includes illustrating how models trained on the PTB-XL dataset can be successfully adapted to perform well on other, smaller ECG datasets, thereby highlighting the method's practicality and advantages in real-world applications."},
{"hidden_stratification": "Hidden stratification is an underlying, often unnoticed structure or pattern within a dataset that can significantly influence analysis outcomes. In diagnostic datasets, it refers to subgroups within a larger category that may experience different levels of algorithmic performance, which are not evident when evaluating the broader category. This phenomenon is particularly relevant in hierarchical organization of diagnostic labels and can affect the accuracy of diagnostic models, especially in clinical applications."},
{"hidden_stratification__evidence_provided__a_first_evaluation_of_the_diagnosis_likelihood_information_provided_within_the_dataset_in_comparison_to_model_uncertainty": "Evidence provided indicates that the research has presented data, results, or findings that support the existence or impact of a particular phenomenon or hypothesis. In this case, it suggests that the study has offered concrete support for the presence of hidden stratification within the dataset."},
