{"fully_connected_classification_head": "A fully connected classification head is a part of a neural network that typically follows feature extraction layers. It consists of one or more fully connected (dense) layers that perform the final classification of the input data based on the features extracted by the preceding layers.|Here is another description: The fully connected classification head is a component used in convolutional models to classify input data after it has been processed by convolutional layers. It is a standard part of neural network architectures designed for classification tasks.|Here is another description: The fully connected classification head is a component of convolutional models that includes a single hidden layer with 128 hidden units. It is used for the final classification task after processing through convolutional layers.|Here is another description: The fully connected classification head is a component used in convolutional models, featuring a single hidden layer with 128 hidden units and batch normalization. It is designed to process the output from convolutional layers and classify the input data."},
