{"dropout_of_0_25_and_0_5_at_the_first_second_fully_connected_layer_respectively": "The dropout of 0.25 and 0.5 at the first and second fully connected layers, respectively, refers to the application of dropout regularization in the classification head. Dropout is a technique used to prevent overfitting by randomly setting a fraction of input units to zero during training. The fractions 0.25 and 0.5 indicate the probability of dropping units in the first and second layers, respectively."},
{"recurrent_neural_networks": "Recurrent neural networks are a class of neural networks designed to handle sequential data by maintaining a form of memory across time steps, allowing them to capture temporal dependencies in the data."},
{"recurrent_neural_networks__consider__unidirectional_and_bidirectional_lstms_and_grus": "The term 'consider' in this context refers to the inclusion and evaluation of specific types of recurrent neural networks as part of a comparative study or experimental setup."},
{"unidirectional_and_bidirectional_lstms_and_grus": "Unidirectional and bidirectional LSTMs (Long Short-Term Memory networks) and GRUs (Gated Recurrent Units) are types of recurrent neural networks. Unidirectional LSTMs and GRUs process input sequences in one direction, while bidirectional variants process input sequences in both forward and backward directions, capturing dependencies from both past and future contexts."},
