{"ways_of_leveraging_further_metadata_within_ptb_xl_to_construct_age_and_sex_prediction_models": "The object refers to the specific methods or approaches discussed in Section III-D, which involve using additional metadata within the PTB-XL dataset to build models that predict age and sex. This involves analyzing and applying the extra information available in PTB-XL to create predictive models."},
{"primary_performance_metric": "The primary performance metric is the main measure used to evaluate the effectiveness of classification experiments. It provides a comprehensive assessment of the model's performance across different classes."},
{"primary_performance_metric__is__macro_averaged_area_under_the_receiver_operating_characteristic_curve_auc": "The predicate \"is\" signifies that the primary performance metric is specifically identified as the macro-averaged area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC). This identification highlights the chosen method for evaluating classification performance."},
{"macro_averaged_area_under_the_receiver_operating_characteristic_curve_auc": "The macro-averaged area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) is a performance metric that averages the AUC values across all classes. This approach is particularly useful in scenarios with class imbalance, as it ensures that each class contributes equally to the overall performance assessment."},
{"auc": "AUC, or Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve, is a performance metric used in classification experiments to evaluate the discriminative power of a classification algorithm. It is particularly useful in scenarios where class imbalance is expected and where a more complete picture of the algorithm's performance is desired without the influence of threshold optimization.|Here is another description: AUC, or Area Under the Curve, is a performance measurement for classification problems at various thresholds settings. It represents the degree or measure of separability, indicating how much the model can distinguish between classes."},
{"auc__is_obtained_by__averaging_class_wise_aucs_over_all_classes": "The phrase \"is obtained by\" indicates the method or process through which the subject (AUC) is calculated or derived. In this context, it specifies that the AUC value is the result of a specific computational process involving class-wise AUCs."},
{"averaging_class_wise_aucs_over_all_classes": "Averaging class-wise AUCs over all classes refers to the process of calculating the AUC for each individual class in a multi-class classification problem and then taking the average of these values. This approach, known as macro-averaging, is used to obtain a single AUC value that represents the overall performance of the classification algorithm across all classes, especially in scenarios where class imbalance is a concern."},
{"metrics": "Metrics in this context refer to performance indicators used to evaluate the effectiveness of classification algorithms. They are crucial for assessing how well a model distinguishes between different classes in a dataset."},
{"metrics__can_be_evaluated_based_on__soft_classifier_outputs": "The phrase \"can be evaluated based on\" indicates a dependency relationship where the effectiveness or value of the subject (metrics) is determined by the object (soft classifier outputs). This means that the metrics' performance is contingent upon the outputs generated by the soft classifier."},
{"soft_classifier_outputs": "Soft classifier outputs are the raw, unthresholded predictions generated by a classification algorithm. These outputs typically represent probabilities or confidence scores for each class, providing a continuous measure of likelihood rather than a discrete class assignment."},
{"threshold_optimization": "Threshold optimization refers to the process of determining the optimal threshold value for a classifier to maximize its performance, often tailored to specific requirements such as clinical needs.|Here is another description: Threshold optimization refers to the process of determining the optimal threshold value for a classifier to maximize its performance. In the context of medical diagnosis, this involves fine-tuning the threshold to balance sensitivity and specificity according to the needs of the clinical application."},
{"threshold_optimization__is_disentangled_from__the_selection_of_an_appropriate_classifier": "The phrase \"is disentangled from\" indicates that the process of threshold optimization is separated or isolated from another process, allowing each to be considered independently."},
{"the_selection_of_an_appropriate_classifier": "The selection of an appropriate classifier involves choosing the best model or algorithm for a classification task based on its performance metrics and suitability for the specific application context."},
{"threshold_optimization__will_have_to_be_adjusted_to__match_the_clinical_requirements": "The phrase \"will have to be adjusted to\" indicates a necessary modification or calibration of the threshold optimization process. This adjustment is required to ensure that the classifier's performance aligns with specific clinical needs and requirements, rather than merely optimizing a general performance metric."},
{"match_the_clinical_requirements": "Matching the clinical requirements means aligning the performance of the classifier with the specific needs and standards of medical practice. This involves ensuring that the classifier's outputs are relevant and useful in a clinical context, taking into account factors such as patient safety, diagnostic accuracy, and the consequences of false positives and false negatives."},
{"macro_averaging": "Macro-averaging is a method used in performance evaluation of classification algorithms, where it calculates the average of metrics (like AUC) across all classes, treating each class equally regardless of its size."},
{"macro_averaging__is_preferred_in__our_setting": "The phrase \"is preferred in\" indicates a recommendation or a choice made within a specific context or setting, suggesting that one method or approach is deemed more suitable or advantageous compared to others."},
{"our_setting": "Our setting refers to the specific environment or conditions under which the classification experiments are conducted, characterized by the expectation of class imbalance and the desire to evaluate classifier performance without bias towards larger classes.|Here is another description: \"Our setting\" refers to the specific environment, conditions, or framework within which the classification experiments are conducted. This setting is characterized by the use of macro-averaged AUC as the primary performance metric and the consideration of soft classifier outputs, among other factors."},
{"class_imbalance": "Class imbalance refers to a scenario in classification tasks where the distribution of examples across different classes is uneven, meaning some classes have significantly more instances than others. This imbalance can affect the performance of classification algorithms, potentially leading to biased results."},
{"class_imbalance__is_expected_in__our_setting": "The phrase \"is expected in\" indicates a prediction or assumption that a certain condition or characteristic will be present within a specified context or environment. In this case, it suggests that class imbalance is anticipated as a normal or common occurrence in the described setting."},
{"score": "The score refers to a metric or evaluation measure used in the context of multi-label classification problems. It is a quantitative value that reflects the performance or accuracy of a classification model."},
{"score__to_be_dominated_by__a_few_large_classes": "The phrase \"to be dominated by\" indicates a situation where the score is heavily influenced or controlled by a specific factor. In this case, it suggests that the score is significantly affected by the presence of a few large classes in the dataset."},
{"a_few_large_classes": "A few large classes refer to a subset of classes within the dataset that have a disproportionately large number of instances or samples compared to other classes. These large classes have a significant impact on the overall distribution and characteristics of the dataset."},
{"distribution_of_pathologies": "The distribution of pathologies refers to how different medical conditions or diseases are represented within a specific dataset. This distribution can vary based on the data collection methods and the population from which the data is sourced.|Here is another description: The distribution of pathologies refers to how different medical conditions or diseases are represented within a dataset. This distribution can vary significantly depending on various factors, including the population studied and the methods used to collect data."},
{"distribution_of_pathologies__does_not_follow__the_natural_distribution_in_the_population": "The phrase \"does not follow\" indicates a discrepancy or deviation between the expected or natural pattern and the observed pattern in the dataset. In this context, it highlights that the distribution of pathologies in the dataset is not aligned with what would be naturally expected in the general population."},
{"the_natural_distribution_in_the_population": "The natural distribution in the population refers to the expected or typical way in which pathologies are distributed among people in a general population. This distribution is influenced by various factors such as genetics, environment, and lifestyle, and it serves as a benchmark for comparing the distribution observed in specific datasets."},
{"distribution_of_pathologies__reflects__the_data_collection_process": "Reflects indicates that the distribution of pathologies is a direct outcome or representation of another process. In this context, it suggests that the way pathologies are distributed in the dataset is influenced by external factors, specifically the data collection process."},
{"the_data_collection_process": "The data collection process encompasses the methods, procedures, and criteria used to gather and compile data for the dataset. This process can significantly influence the resulting distribution of pathologies, as it may introduce biases or reflect specific characteristics of the population or environment from which the data was collected."},
{"metrics_for_multi_label_classification_problems": "Metrics for multi-label classification problems refer to the various methods and criteria used to evaluate the performance of models that classify items into multiple categories simultaneously. These metrics are crucial for assessing how well a model can handle complex classification tasks where an instance can belong to more than one class."},
{"metrics_for_multi_label_classification_problems__are__a_wide_field": "The predicate \"are\" signifies the state or condition of the subject, indicating that metrics for multi-label classification problems exist as a broad and extensive area of study. It highlights the comprehensive nature of the field, encompassing a variety of approaches and techniques."},
{"a_wide_field": "A wide field denotes an extensive and diverse area of research or application, characterized by a large number of different methods, theories, and practices. In the context of metrics for multi-label classification problems, it suggests that there are numerous ways to evaluate model performance, each with its own strengths and applications."},
{"review_on_multi_label_classification_metrics_and_algorithms": "A comprehensive review that delves into the various metrics and algorithms used for multi-label classification, providing insights into how these methods can be applied and evaluated."},
