{"one": "The subject refers to a fundamental concept or entity within the context of multi-label classification metrics and algorithms. It is a basic element that serves as a starting point for further classification and analysis.|Here is another description: The number \"one\" in this context represents the total sum to which the output probabilities are normalized. This ensures that the probabilities are valid and interpretable as they sum up to a complete certainty, reflecting the exhaustive nature of the classification outcomes."},
{"one__distinguishes__sample_centric_and_label_centric_metrics_such_as_macro_auc": "The predicate indicates the action or relationship that the subject has with the object. In this case, it describes the process of differentiating or categorizing metrics based on their focus or orientation."},
{"sample_centric_and_label_centric_metrics_such_as_macro_auc": "The object represents the two types of metrics that are distinguished by the subject: sample-centric and label-centric metrics. These metrics, such as macro AUC, are methods used to evaluate the performance of multi-label classification models, focusing either on individual samples or on specific labels within the dataset."},
{"sample_centric_metrics": "Sample-centric metrics are a type of evaluation metric used in multi-label classification problems, focusing on the performance of the model on individual samples rather than on labels."},
{"sample_centric_metrics__see__e_g_48": "The predicate \"see\" in this context directs the reader to reference additional information or examples that elaborate on the subject, in this case, sample-centric metrics."},
{"e_g_48": "The object \"e.g. [48]\" refers to a specific example or reference, likely a paper or section within a document, that provides further explanation or examples of sample-centric metrics."},
{"results_for_sample_centric_metrics": "The results for sample-centric metrics refer to the evaluation outcomes based on metrics that focus on individual samples within a dataset, as opposed to label-centric metrics which aggregate performance across labels."},
