{"the_code_repository": "The code repository is a storage location where the source code, along with associated documentation and resources, is maintained. In this context, it serves as the place where the results for sample-centric metrics can be found."},
{"icbeb2018": "ICBEB2018 is a dataset that has been utilized for various performance evaluations in the context of machine learning challenges. It is specifically noted for its use in reporting performance metrics such as macro-averaged F\u03b2-score and G\u03b2-score.|Here is another description: ICBEB2018 is a dataset that was selected as the training dataset for the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020. It is used for evaluating performance metrics in machine learning classification tasks, particularly in the context of medical data analysis.|Here is another description: ICBEB2018 is a dataset used for training and validating classifiers in the context of medical data analysis. It serves as a benchmark for evaluating the performance of different classification algorithms.|Here is another description: ICBEB2018 is a dataset used for evaluating the classification performance of various machine learning models, particularly in the context of medical data analysis. It serves as an independent validation set to confirm the results obtained from another dataset, PTB-XL, ensuring the robustness and generalizability of the classifiers' performance.|Here is another description: The object, 'ICBEB2018', is a dataset used for fine-tuning the models. It is part of an experiment to evaluate how models perform when trained on varying sizes of data, particularly in the small dataset regime."},
{"icbeb2018__was_selected_as__training_dataset_for_the_physionet_cinc_challenge_2020": "The predicate \"was selected as\" indicates that ICBEB2018 was chosen or designated for a specific purpose or role. In this context, it signifies that ICBEB2018 was chosen to serve as a training dataset."},
{"training_dataset_for_the_physionet_cinc_challenge_2020": "The PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020 is a competition or event where participants develop and test algorithms for physiological signal processing and analysis. ICBEB2018 was selected as the training dataset for this challenge, implying it was used to train and evaluate the performance of algorithms submitted by participants."},
{"icbeb2018__reports__two_further_performance_metrics": "The predicate'reports' indicates the action of providing or presenting specific information or metrics. In this context, it means that the dataset ICBEB2018 is being used to present or provide two additional performance metrics for evaluation purposes."},
{"two_further_performance_metrics": "The two further performance metrics reported for ICBEB2018 are a macro-averaged F\u03b2-score (with \u03b2=2) and a macro-averaged G\u03b2-score (with \u03b2=2). These metrics are used to evaluate the performance of classification algorithms, with the F\u03b2-score emphasizing recall over precision and the G\u03b2-score providing a different balance between true positives, false positives, and false negatives.|Here is another description: Two additional performance metrics were reported for the ICBEB2018 dataset, which was used as a training dataset for the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020. These metrics were chosen for comparability purposes."},
{"two_further_performance_metrics__were_used_as__evaluation_metrics_during_the_first_stage_of_the_challenge": "The predicate 'were used as' indicates that the two performance metrics served a specific role or function within the context of the challenge. In this case, they were employed to evaluate the performance of the algorithms during the first stage of the challenge."},
{"evaluation_metrics_during_the_first_stage_of_the_challenge": "Evaluation metrics during the first stage of the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020 included a macro-averaged F\u03b2-score (\u03b2 = 2) and a macro-averaged G\u03b2-score with \u03b2 = 2. These metrics were used to assess the performance of the algorithms, with sample weights chosen inversely proportional to the number of labels."},
{"macro_averaged_f\u03b2_score": "The macro-averaged F\u03b2-score is a performance metric used in the evaluation of classification algorithms, particularly in the context of the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020. It is calculated by averaging the F\u03b2-scores across all classes, with each class weighted equally regardless of its frequency in the dataset."},
{"macro_averaged_f\u03b2_score__has__\u03b2_2": "The predicate 'has' in this context indicates that the macro-averaged F\u03b2-score is characterized by a specific value of \u03b2, which influences the balance between precision and recall in the calculation of the F\u03b2-score."},
{"\u03b2_2": "The value \u03b2 = 2 in the context of the macro-averaged F\u03b2-score signifies that the score places a higher emphasis on recall over precision. This is because a \u03b2 value greater than 1 assigns more weight to recall, making it a suitable metric when the cost of false negatives is considered higher than that of false positives.|Here is another description: The object \u03b2 = 2 represents the value of the parameter \u03b2 used in the calculation of the macro-averaged G\u03b2-score. A \u03b2 value greater than 1 assigns more weight to recall than precision, which can be a desirable property in certain classification tasks."},
{"macro_averaged_g\u03b2_score": "The macro-averaged G\u03b2-score is a performance metric used in evaluating classification algorithms, specifically in the context of the ICBEB2018 dataset and the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020. It is calculated by averaging the G\u03b2-scores across all classes, with sample weights chosen inversely proportional to the number of labels."},
{"macro_averaged_g\u03b2_score__has__\u03b2_2": "The predicate \"has\" in this context indicates that the macro-averaged G\u03b2-score is characterized by a specific parameter value, \u03b2 = 2. This parameter influences the weight assigned to recall versus precision in the calculation of the G\u03b2-score."},
{"g\u03b2": "G\u03b2 is a macro-averaged performance metric used in the evaluation of classification algorithms, particularly in the context of the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020. It is designed to balance the trade-off between precision and recall, with a parameter \u03b2 that allows for assigning more weight to recall when \u03b2 > 1."},
{"g\u03b2__is_defined_as__tp_tp_fp_\u03b2_fn": "The predicate \"is defined as\" specifies the mathematical formulation of G\u03b2, which is expressed as TP/(TP + FP + \u03b2 \u00b7 FN). This definition encapsulates the relationship between true positives (TP), false positives (FP), and false negatives (FN), adjusted by the parameter \u03b2 to emphasize recall over precision."},
{"tp_tp_fp_\u03b2_fn": "TP/(TP + FP + \u03b2 \u00b7 FN) is the mathematical expression that defines the G\u03b2 score. It calculates the ratio of true positives to the sum of true positives, false positives, and a weighted count of false negatives, where the weight is determined by the parameter \u03b2. This formula is used to evaluate the performance of classification algorithms, particularly in scenarios where recall is more critical than precision."},
{"sample_weights": "Sample weights refer to the values assigned to individual samples in a dataset to influence the learning process of a model. In the context of the ICBEB2018 dataset used for the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020, these weights are crucial for balancing the impact of samples during training."},
{"sample_weights__are_chosen__inversely_proportional_to_the_number_of_labels": "The predicate \"are chosen\" indicates the method or criteria used to determine the sample weights. In this scenario, the weights are selected based on a specific rule that relates to the number of labels associated with each sample."},
{"inversely_proportional_to_the_number_of_labels": "The object \"inversely proportional to the number of labels\" describes the specific criterion used for choosing sample weights. This means that samples with fewer labels receive higher weights, while those with more labels receive lower weights, aiming to balance the influence of samples with different label counts during model training."},
{"values_of_\u03b2_1": "Values of \u03b2 greater than 1 refer to a specific setting in performance metrics like the F\u03b2-score and G\u03b2-score, where \u03b2 is a parameter that adjusts the balance between precision and recall."},
{"values_of_\u03b2_1__allow_to_assign__more_weight_to_recall_than_precision": "Allowing to assign indicates the capability or permission to allocate or distribute a certain emphasis or importance in a specified manner within the context of performance metrics."},
{"more_weight_to_recall_than_precision": "More weight to recall than precision signifies a scenario where the evaluation metric places greater importance on the ability of a model to correctly identify all relevant instances (recall) over its ability to avoid labeling irrelevant instances as relevant (precision)."},
{"assigning_more_weight_to_recall_than_precision": "Assigning more weight to recall than precision refers to a strategy in performance evaluation where the emphasis is placed more on the ability of a model to correctly identify all relevant instances (recall) rather than on minimizing the number of false positives (precision)."},
{"assigning_more_weight_to_recall_than_precision__might_be__a_desirable_property": "\"Might be\" indicates a possibility or potential scenario where assigning more weight to recall than precision could be considered beneficial or advantageous under certain conditions or for specific applications."},
{"a_desirable_property": "A desirable property in this context means that assigning more weight to recall than precision could be advantageous, particularly in scenarios where it is more critical to capture all relevant cases even at the cost of including some false positives."},
{"applying_this_equally_to_the_norm_class": "The subject refers to the application of a method or metric, specifically a macro-averaged _F\u03b2_ -score and _G\u03b2_ -score with _\u03b2_ = 2, to the _NORM_ -class in the context of evaluating performance metrics for a dataset."},
{"applying_this_equally_to_the_norm_class__seems__questionable": "The predicate \"seems\" indicates a subjective evaluation or opinion about the appropriateness or validity of applying the aforementioned method or metric to the _NORM_ -class."},
{"questionable": "The object \"questionable\" describes the nature of the evaluation, suggesting that there are doubts or concerns about the suitability of applying the method or metric equally to the _NORM_ -class, particularly regarding the need for high precision in this context."},
{"high_precision": "High precision refers to the requirement for accurate and reliable classification results, particularly in sensitive applications where false positives can have significant consequences."},
{"high_precision__is_required__in_the_case_of_the_norm_class": "Is required indicates a necessary condition or standard that must be met for effective performance in a specific context, highlighting the importance of precision in certain applications."},
{"in_the_case_of_the_norm_class": "The NORM-class represents a category within a classification system where maintaining high precision is critical, possibly due to the nature of the data or the implications of misclassification."},
{"corresponding_scores": "The corresponding scores refer to the macro-averaged _F\u03b2_ -score and macro-averaged _G\u03b2_ -score used as performance metrics in the evaluation of classification algorithms."},
{"corresponding_scores__are_sensitive_to__the_chosen_classification_threshold": "The sensitivity of the corresponding scores to the chosen classification threshold indicates that the performance metrics are influenced by the threshold value selected for distinguishing between different classes in the classification task."},
{"the_chosen_classification_threshold": "The chosen classification threshold is the specific value set to determine the boundary between different classes in a classification algorithm, which in this context, affects the macro-averaged _F\u03b2_ -score and macro-averaged _G\u03b2_ -score."},
{"classification_threshold": "The classification threshold is a critical value used in binary classification to determine the class of a given input. It is the point at which the classifier decides whether an input belongs to one class or another, based on the output score."},
{"classification_threshold__is_determined_by__maximizing_the_f\u03b2_g\u03b2_score_on_the_training_set": "The phrase \"is determined by\" indicates the method or criterion used to set the classification threshold. In this context, it refers to the process of selecting the optimal threshold value based on a specific performance metric."},
{"maximizing_the_f\u03b2_g\u03b2_score_on_the_training_set": "Maximizing the F\u03b2 / G\u03b2-score on the training set involves adjusting the classification threshold to achieve the highest possible value of these metrics. The F\u03b2-score is a measure of a test's accuracy, considering both the precision and recall of the test, while the G\u03b2-score is a variant that also considers the false positives. Maximizing these scores ensures that the classifier performs well in terms of both precision and recall, with an emphasis on recall when \u03b2 > 1.|Here is another description: Maximizing the F\u03b2 / G\u03b2-score on the training set refers to the process of adjusting the classification threshold to achieve the highest possible F\u03b2 or G\u03b2-score during the training phase of a machine learning model. This is done to optimize the balance between precision and recall, with a higher \u03b2 value giving more weight to recall."},
{"maximizing_the_f\u03b2_g\u03b2_score_on_the_training_set__is__an_undesirable_aspect": "\"Is\" serves as the linking verb in this context, connecting the subject (maximizing the F\u03b2 / G\u03b2-score on the training set) to its characterization (an undesirable aspect). It establishes that the subject has the quality or characteristic described by the object."},
{"an_undesirable_aspect": "An undesirable aspect in this context refers to a negative characteristic or consequence associated with maximizing the F\u03b2 / G\u03b2-score on the training set. Specifically, it indicates that this practice entangles the discriminative performance of the classification algorithm with the process of threshold determination, which is considered unfavorable."},
{"undesirable_aspect": "The undesirable aspect refers to a negative characteristic or feature within a system or process that complicates or negatively impacts its performance or outcomes."},
{"undesirable_aspect__entangles__the_discriminative_performance_of_the_classification_algorithm_with_the_process_of_threshold_determination": "Entangles describes a situation where two or more elements are intricately connected or mixed together in a way that makes it difficult to separate or distinguish their individual effects or contributions."},
{"the_discriminative_performance_of_the_classification_algorithm_with_the_process_of_threshold_determination": "The discriminative performance of the classification algorithm with the process of threshold determination refers to how well the algorithm can distinguish between different classes or categories, and the method used to set a threshold value that determines the classification decision."},
{"95_confidence_intervals": "95% confidence intervals are statistical measures used to express the uncertainty or variability in the results of a statistical analysis. They provide a range of values within which the true value of a parameter is expected to lie with a certain level of confidence."},
{"95_confidence_intervals__are_provided_via__empirical_bootstrapping_on_the_test_set": "The phrase \"are provided via\" indicates the method or process through which the 95% confidence intervals are obtained. In this context, it specifies that the confidence intervals are generated using a particular technique."},
{"empirical_bootstrapping_on_the_test_set": "Empirical bootstrapping on the test set is a resampling technique used to estimate the distribution of a statistic by repeatedly sampling with replacement from the original dataset. In this case, it is applied to the test set to generate 95% confidence intervals, providing a measure of the uncertainty in the performance metrics of classifiers."},
{"empirical_bootstrapping": "Empirical bootstrapping is a statistical method used to estimate the distribution of a statistic by resampling with replacement from the original data. It is often used to assess the uncertainty of estimates and to construct confidence intervals."},
{"empirical_bootstrapping__is_done_with__10_000_iterations": "The phrase \"is done with\" indicates the number of iterations used in the empirical bootstrapping process. In this context, it specifies the computational effort and the robustness of the bootstrapping method employed."},
{"10_000_iterations": "10,000 iterations refer to the number of times the resampling process is repeated in the empirical bootstrapping method. This large number of iterations helps in obtaining a stable and reliable estimate of the statistic\u2019s distribution."},
{"point_estimate": "The point estimate refers to a single value that serves as the best estimate of a parameter, derived from evaluating the performance of classifiers on a dataset. In the context of the provided text, it represents the central value of performance metrics such as _F\u03b2_ -score and _G\u03b2_ -score obtained from the entire test set."},
{"point_estimate__is_reported_from__evaluating_on_the_whole_test_set": "The phrase \"is reported from\" indicates the source or method by which the point estimate is obtained. It signifies that the point estimate is derived or calculated based on the evaluation conducted on the whole test set."},
{"evaluating_on_the_whole_test_set": "Evaluating on the whole test set means assessing the performance of classifiers using all available data in the test set. This process provides a comprehensive measure of how well the classifiers perform on unseen data, which is critical for understanding their generalizability and effectiveness."},
{"lower_and_upper_confidence_intervals": "The lower and upper confidence intervals are statistical measures used to express the uncertainty around a point estimate, providing a range within which the true value is expected to lie with a certain level of confidence."},
{"lower_and_upper_confidence_intervals__are_estimated_using__the_bootstrap_examples": "The phrase \"are estimated using\" indicates the method or process through which the lower and upper confidence intervals are calculated. In this context, it specifies that the bootstrap examples are the means by which these intervals are derived."},
{"the_bootstrap_examples": "The bootstrap examples refer to the multiple resampled datasets created through the bootstrapping method. This technique involves randomly drawing samples with replacement from the original dataset to generate new datasets, which are then used to estimate statistics such as confidence intervals."},
{"non_overlapping_confidence_intervals": "Non-overlapping confidence intervals refer to the statistical method used to assess the uncertainty of classifiers' scores by providing a range within which the true value of a parameter is expected to lie with a certain level of confidence. In this context, they are derived through empirical bootstrapping on the test set, with 10,000 iterations."},
{"non_overlapping_confidence_intervals__signify__statistically_significant_differences_between_the_classifiers": "The predicate \"signify\" in this context means to indicate or denote as a sign or signal. Here, it is used to describe how non-overlapping confidence intervals serve as an indicator of statistically significant differences between the performance of different classifiers."},
{"statistically_significant_differences_between_the_classifiers": "Statistically significant differences between the classifiers refer to the meaningful distinctions in performance metrics among various classification algorithms. These differences are considered significant when the probability of observing such differences by chance is low, typically below a threshold such as 0.05.|Here is another description: Statistically significant differences between classifiers refer to the scenario where the performance metrics of two or more classifiers are sufficiently distinct that the observed differences are unlikely to have occurred by random chance. This significance is typically assessed using statistical tests or by examining whether the confidence intervals of the performance metrics overlap or not."},
{"overlapping_confidence_intervals": "Overlapping confidence intervals refer to the ranges within which the true value of a statistical measure is expected to lie with a certain level of confidence, based on the sample data. In the context of evaluating classifiers, these intervals are used to assess the uncertainty and variability of performance metrics."},
{"overlapping_confidence_intervals__do_not_signify__statistically_significant_differences_between_the_classifiers": "The phrase \"do not signify\" indicates that the presence of overlapping confidence intervals between different classifiers does not automatically imply a particular conclusion about their performance. Specifically, it means that overlapping intervals alone are not sufficient to determine whether there are statistically significant differences between the classifiers."},
{"bootstrap_estimates_of_the_difference": "Bootstrap estimates of the difference refer to statistical measures calculated through the bootstrap method to determine the variation or uncertainty in the difference between two classifiers' performance metrics."},
{"bootstrap_estimates_of_the_difference__are_calculated_for__the_best_performing_and_all_other_classifiers": "The phrase \"are calculated for\" indicates the process of performing computations or statistical analyses specifically targeted towards a defined set of entities, in this case, classifiers."},
{"the_best_performing_and_all_other_classifiers": "The best-performing and all other classifiers refer to a set of machine learning models where one is identified as having the highest performance, and the rest are compared against this top performer to evaluate their relative effectiveness."},
{"confidence_intervals_for_the_difference": "The confidence intervals for the difference refer to the statistical measure used to assess the uncertainty of the difference between the best-performing classifier and other classifiers in the evaluation. These intervals are derived through empirical bootstrapping on the test set."},
{"confidence_intervals_for_the_difference__do_not_cover__zero": "The predicate \"do not cover\" indicates that the confidence intervals calculated for the difference between classifiers do not include the value zero. This suggests a statistically significant difference between the classifiers at a confidence level of 0.05."},
{"zero": "Zero in this context represents the null value or no difference between the classifiers being compared. If the confidence intervals for the difference do not cover zero, it implies that there is a significant difference between the classifiers."},
{"two_classifiers": "The two classifiers are models evaluated in the context of the ICBEB2018 dataset, used for the PhysioNet/CinC challenge 2020. Their performance is assessed using various metrics including macro-averaged F\u03b2-score and G\u03b2-score."},
{"two_classifiers__are_considered_statistically_significant_at_a_confidence_level_of__0_05": "The predicate indicates that the performance difference between the two classifiers has been statistically evaluated. This evaluation uses bootstrapping methods to determine if the difference in their scores is significant, based on confidence intervals that do not include zero."},
