{"predictions__propagated_from__experiment_diag_upwards_the_hierarchy_over_sub_diag_to_super_diag": "Propagated from indicates the process by which predictions are transferred or moved from one level to another within a hierarchical structure, specifically from a lower level (sub-diagnostic) to a higher level (super-diagnostic)."},
{"experiment_diag_upwards_the_hierarchy_over_sub_diag_to_super_diag": "Experiment _diag._ upwards the hierarchy over _sub-diag._ to _super-diag._ describes a specific experimental setup where predictions are aggregated and moved from sub-diagnostic levels to super-diagnostic levels within a hierarchical diagnostic label system."},
{"prediction_probabilities": "Prediction probabilities refer to the likelihood values assigned to different outcomes or classes in a predictive model. In the context of hierarchical diagnostic labels, these probabilities are generated for each diagnostic ECG statement at various levels of classification."},
{"prediction_probabilities__summed_up__of_the_corresponding_child_nodes": "The action of summing up involves aggregating or combining prediction probabilities from multiple sources or levels within a hierarchical structure. This process is used to propagate predictions upwards in the hierarchy, ensuring that the probabilities reflect the collective likelihood across different classification levels."},
{"of_the_corresponding_child_nodes": "The corresponding child nodes in a hierarchical structure represent the lower-level classifications or subcategories within the diagnostic labels. Their prediction probabilities are aggregated to provide a comprehensive probability value for higher-level classifications, such as superclasses."},
{"output_probabilities": "Output probabilities refer to the likelihood values generated by a predictive model for different possible outcomes. In the context of hierarchical diagnostic labels, these probabilities are calculated for each diagnostic statement and aggregated across different levels of the hierarchy."},
{"output_probabilities__limited_to__one": "The phrase \"limited to\" indicates a constraint or boundary placed on the output probabilities. In this context, it means that the sum of the probabilities across different diagnostic labels is constrained to ensure they do not exceed a total of one, maintaining the probabilistic integrity of the predictions."},
{"experiments__conducted_with__other_aggregation_strategies_such_as_using_the_maximum_or_the_mean_of_the_predictions_of_the_child_nodes": "The phrase \"conducted with\" indicates the manner in which the experiments were performed. It suggests that the experiments incorporated various methods or strategies to aggregate predictions, specifically exploring different approaches to combine the results from child nodes in the hierarchical structure."},
{"other_aggregation_strategies_such_as_using_the_maximum_or_the_mean_of_the_predictions_of_the_child_nodes": "Other aggregation strategies such as using the maximum or the mean of the predictions of the child nodes refer to alternative methods tested in the experiments to combine prediction probabilities. These strategies involve either taking the highest prediction probability or averaging the probabilities from the child nodes in the hierarchical structure of diagnostic labels."},
{"other_aggregation_strategies": "Other aggregation strategies refer to alternative methods used to combine prediction probabilities from child nodes in a hierarchical structure, such as using the maximum or the mean of these predictions."},
{"other_aggregation_strategies__observed__only_minor_impact_on_the_results": "Observed indicates that the impact of using different aggregation strategies on the results was examined and noted during the experiments."},
{"only_minor_impact_on_the_results": "Only minor impact on the results signifies that the alternative aggregation strategies, such as using the maximum or the mean, did not significantly alter the outcomes compared to the default method of summing up prediction probabilities."},
{"models__trained_on__the_specific_level": "The phrase \"trained on\" indicates that the models have undergone a learning process using a specific dataset or level of data. This training allows the models to make predictions based on the patterns and relationships they have learned from the data."},
{"the_specific_level": "\"The specific level\" refers to a particular tier or category within the hierarchical organization of diagnostic labels used in the PTB-XL dataset. This level likely corresponds to one of the 23 subclasses where the models were trained to recognize and predict outcomes based on the ECG data assigned to these subclasses."},
{"fig_4__shows__hierarchical_decomposition_of_class_specific_aucs": "The predicate \"shows\" indicates that Fig. 4 visually presents or illustrates the hierarchical decomposition of class-specific AUCs, allowing viewers to understand the relationships and stratifications within the data."},
{"hierarchical_decomposition_of_class_specific_aucs": "The hierarchical decomposition of class-specific AUCs refers to the breakdown of Area Under the Curve (AUC) metrics for different classes into subclasses and individual diagnostic statements. This decomposition reveals hidden stratifications, where certain diagnostic subpopulations may exhibit inferior algorithmic performance that is not apparent when only considering the overall superior performance of the superclass."},
{"hierarchical_decomposition": "Hierarchical decomposition is a method used to break down complex data into more manageable and interpretable components. In the context of evaluating algorithmic performance, it helps reveal hidden stratifications within data."},
{"hierarchical_decomposition__decomposes__class_specific_aucs_onto_subclasses_and_individual_diagnostic_statements": "The predicate \"decomposes\" indicates the action of breaking down or separating a complex entity into its constituent parts. In this context, it refers to the process of dividing class-specific AUCs into more detailed subclasses and individual diagnostic statements."},
{"class_specific_aucs_onto_subclasses_and_individual_diagnostic_statements": "Class-specific AUCs (Area Under the Curve) are metrics used to evaluate the performance of classification algorithms for specific classes within a dataset. When decomposed onto subclasses and individual diagnostic statements, these AUCs provide a more granular view of algorithmic performance, highlighting any hidden stratifications or inferior performance on certain subpopulations."},
{"diagnostic_statements__exhibit__hidden_stratification": "The predicate \"exhibit\" in this context means to display or show a particular characteristic or behavior. Here, it indicates that certain diagnostic statements reveal or demonstrate a specific phenomenon."},
{"hidden_stratification__means__inferior_algorithmic_performance_on_certain_diagnostic_subpopulations": "The term \"means\" in this context signifies that hidden stratification directly results in or is synonymous with inferior algorithmic performance on specific diagnostic subpopulations."},
{"inferior_algorithmic_performance_on_certain_diagnostic_subpopulations": "Inferior algorithmic performance on certain diagnostic subpopulations indicates that the algorithm does not perform as well for these specific groups compared to the overall or superclass performance, highlighting a disparity in effectiveness."},
{"inferior_algorithmic_performance": "Inferior algorithmic performance refers to the subpar effectiveness of an algorithm when applied to specific diagnostic subpopulations within a dataset. This underperformance is a critical aspect to consider in evaluating the overall capability of the algorithm.|Here is another description: Inferior algorithmic performance refers to the situation where a model or algorithm performs less effectively on certain subsets of data compared to others. This can be particularly problematic in clinical applications where accurate diagnosis is critical."},
{"inferior_algorithmic_performance__remains__hidden_when_considering_only_the_superior_superclass_performance": "The predicate \"remains\" indicates that the inferior algorithmic performance is persistent and does not improve or become apparent even under certain conditions or evaluations. It suggests a consistent issue that is not easily resolved or detected through standard assessments."},
{"hidden_when_considering_only_the_superior_superclass_performance": "The object \"hidden when considering only the superior superclass performance\" describes a situation where the overall performance of an algorithm appears excellent when evaluated at a higher, more general level (superclass). This masks the inferior performance on specific subpopulations, making it difficult to identify and address the underlying issues without a more detailed analysis."},
