{"fig_4__shows__hierarchical_decomposition_tree_like_structure_for_the_diagnostic_labels_in_sub_and_superclasses": "The predicate 'shows' indicates that Fig. 4 is presenting or displaying information. In this context, it is used to convey that the figure is illustrating the hierarchical decomposition of diagnostic labels."},
{"hierarchical_decomposition_tree_like_structure_for_the_diagnostic_labels_in_sub_and_superclasses": "The hierarchical decomposition (tree-like structure) for the diagnostic labels in sub- and superclasses is a method of organizing diagnostic ECG statements into a tree structure. This structure allows for the categorization of labels into broader superclasses and more specific subclasses, reflecting an ontology that can be used to understand and predict ECG statements."},
{"predictions__propagated_from__experiment_diag_upwards_the_hierarchy_over_sub_diag_to_super_diag": "Propagated from indicates the process by which predictions are transferred or moved from one level to another within a hierarchical structure, specifically from a lower level (sub-diagnostic) to a higher level (super-diagnostic)."},
{"experiment_diag_upwards_the_hierarchy_over_sub_diag_to_super_diag": "Experiment _diag._ upwards the hierarchy over _sub-diag._ to _super-diag._ describes the method of moving predictions from the experiment diagnostic level up through the hierarchy, from sub-diagnostic to super-diagnostic levels, by summing up prediction probabilities of child nodes and limiting the output probabilities to one."},
{"output_probabilities": "Output probabilities refer to the likelihood values generated by a predictive model for different outcomes or classes. In the context of hierarchical classification of ECG statements, these probabilities are calculated for each diagnostic label at various levels of the hierarchy."},
{"output_probabilities__limited_to__one": "The phrase \"limited to\" indicates a constraint or boundary applied to the output probabilities. In this context, it means that the sum of the probabilities across all possible outcomes is constrained to equal one, ensuring that the probabilities are properly normalized."},
