{"samples_without_norm_as_additional_label": "Samples without _NORM_ as an additional label are data points that do not have the _NORM_ label assigned to them. This label likely signifies a specific condition or characteristic, and its absence in these samples is a notable feature that distinguishes them from other samples in the dataset."},
{"samples_from_the_high_error_cluster": "The samples from the high error cluster refer to a specific subset of data points that exhibit higher error rates in a classification or prediction model. These samples are part of a larger dataset and are distinguished by their tendency to be misclassified or incorrectly predicted by the model."},
{"samples_from_the_high_error_cluster__are_mostly__samples_with_co_occurring_norm": "The phrase \"are mostly\" indicates a predominant characteristic or composition of the subject. In this context, it suggests that the majority of the samples from the high error cluster share a common attribute or feature, which is the presence of co-occurring _NORM_."},
