{"discriminative_classifier": "A discriminative classifier is a type of machine learning model designed to distinguish between different classes or categories based on input features. In the context of ECG analysis, it is used to classify various ECG statements into specific categories."},
{"discriminative_classifier__has__unreliable_score_estimate_on_test_set": "The predicate \"has\" in this context indicates that the discriminative classifier possesses or is characterized by a certain attribute or property, which in this case is an unreliable score estimate on the test set."},
{"unreliable_score_estimate_on_test_set": "An unreliable score estimate on the test set refers to the performance metric of the discriminative classifier that is not dependable or consistent, likely due to factors such as small sample size or inherent variability in the data."},
{"unreliable_score_estimate": "The unreliable score estimate refers to the inaccuracy or lack of reliability in the performance metrics or scores obtained from a discriminative classifier when applied to a test set. This unreliability is particularly noted in the context of evaluating specific ECG statements."},
{"unreliable_score_estimate__due_to__small_sample_size": "The phrase \"due to\" indicates the cause-and-effect relationship between the unreliable score estimate and the small sample size. It suggests that the unreliability in the score estimate is a direct result of the limited number of samples used in the evaluation."},
{"small_sample_size": "The small sample size refers to the limited number of data points or instances available for testing the discriminative classifier. This scarcity of data points leads to unreliable performance metrics, as the classifier may not have sufficient information to make accurate predictions or assessments."},
{"ecg_statements__stand_out_from__other_members_of_the_same_subclass": "The phrase \"stand out from\" indicates a notable difference or distinction between certain ECG statements and their peers within the same subclass. This distinction suggests that these statements exhibit unique characteristics or performance metrics that set them apart from others in their category."},
{"other_members_of_the_same_subclass": "Other members of the same subclass refer to ECG statements that are grouped together based on similar characteristics or the heart conditions they represent. Within this group, some statements may show different performance levels or exhibit unique traits that differentiate them from the majority."},
{"performance_deficiency": "Performance deficiency refers to the inadequate or subpar performance of a system, process, or individual, often measured against expected standards or outcomes. In the context of machine learning classifiers, it denotes the failure of the classifier to achieve desired accuracy or reliability in its predictions."},
{"performance_deficiency__cannot_only_be_attributed_to__effects_of_small_sample_sizes": "The phrase \"cannot only be attributed to\" indicates that the cause of a particular issue or deficiency is multifaceted and cannot be solely explained by a single factor. It suggests that while one element may contribute to the problem, other factors are also at play, necessitating a more comprehensive analysis."},
{"effects_of_small_sample_sizes": "The effects of small sample sizes refer to the limitations and inaccuracies that arise when statistical analyses or machine learning models are trained and tested on insufficient data. Small sample sizes can lead to unreliable results, overfitting, and a lack of generalizability, impacting the performance and validity of the conclusions drawn."},
{"classes_nst_lao_lae_ivcd": "The subject refers to specific classes of ECG statements, namely _NST__ (non-specific ST changes), _LAO/LAE_ (left atrial overload/enlargement), and _IVCD_ (non-specific intraventricular conduction disturbance (block)). These classes are noted for their weak performance in a discriminative classifier, particularly due to their non-specific nature and potential to subsume heterogeneous groups of findings."},
{"classes_nst_lao_lae_ivcd__are_in__bottom_layer_of_the_hierarchy": "The predicate \"are in\" signifies the position or placement of the subject classes within a hierarchical structure. It indicates that these classes occupy a specific location\u2014the bottom layer\u2014in the hierarchy of ECG statement classification."},
{"bottom_layer_of_the_hierarchy": "The object \"bottom layer of the hierarchy\" describes the lowest level in a structured classification system for ECG statements. Classes placed here are fundamental and specific, and their performance metrics can significantly influence the overall assessment of the classifier's effectiveness."},
