{"models_trained_on_coarse_labels": "Models trained on coarse labels refer to machine learning models that have been trained using broad, generalized categories or labels rather than detailed, specific ones. These models are designed to classify or predict outcomes based on the provided coarse labels."},
{"models_trained_on_coarse_labels__might_hide__clinically_relevant_stratification": "The phrase \"might hide\" indicates a potential consequence or risk associated with the subject. In this context, it suggests that the use of coarse labels in training models could lead to an obscuration or concealment of important details or patterns within the data."},
{"clinically_relevant_stratification": "Clinically relevant stratification refers to the meaningful division or categorization of data based on clinical criteria or patient characteristics that are important for medical decision-making. This stratification is crucial for identifying subgroups within a population that may respond differently to treatments or have distinct clinical outcomes."},
{"ptb_xl_dataset__does_not_provide__any_clinical_ground_truth_on_the_considered_samples": "The phrase \"does not provide\" indicates a limitation or absence within the dataset. In this context, it highlights that the PTB-XL dataset lacks a specific type of information that is typically expected in clinical datasets."},
{"any_clinical_ground_truth_on_the_considered_samples": "\"Any clinical ground truth on the considered samples\" refers to the absence of definitive, clinically verified diagnoses or outcomes for the ECG samples within the PTB-XL dataset. Instead, the dataset offers annotations based solely on the ECG signals, without corresponding clinical validation."},
{"ptb_xl_dataset__provides__cardiologists_annotations_based_on_the_ecg_signal_itself": "The PTB-XL dataset provides cardiologists' annotations, which means it offers expert evaluations and interpretations of the ECG signals included in the dataset."},
{"cardiologists_annotations_based_on_the_ecg_signal_itself": "Cardiologists\u2019 annotations based on the ECG signal itself refer to the expert evaluations and interpretations made by cardiologists solely from the information contained within the ECG recordings, without additional clinical data."},
{"cardiologists_annotations": "Cardiologists\u2019 annotations refer to the evaluations and interpretations made by cardiologists based on the ECG signal itself, without any clinical ground truth provided for the samples."},
{"cardiologists_annotations__could_compromise__the_analysis": "The phrase \"could compromise\" indicates a potential negative impact or risk that the cardiologists\u2019 annotations might have on a subsequent process or outcome."},
{"the_analysis": "The analysis in this context refers to the detailed examination and study of the ECG data, specifically towards identifying subgroups with certain combinations of co-occurring ECG statements or pathologies."},
{"in_depth_study": "An in-depth study refers to a thorough and detailed examination of a particular topic or area of interest. In the context of medical research, it involves a comprehensive analysis aimed at uncovering nuanced insights and understanding complex phenomena.|Here is another description: An in-depth study refers to a thorough and detailed examination of a particular topic or area, in this case, the identification of subgroups with certain combinations of co-occurring ECG statements/pathologies.|Here is another description: An in-depth study refers to a thorough and detailed examination of a particular topic or dataset, in this case, focusing on the identification of subgroups with specific combinations of co-occurring ECG statements or pathologies."},
{"in_depth_study__is_towards__the_identification_of_subgroups_with_certain_combinations_of_co_occurring_ecg_statements_pathologies": "\"Is towards\" indicates a direction or goal that the subject is aiming for or working toward. In this context, it signifies that the in-depth study is specifically focused on achieving a particular objective related to ECG analysis."},
{"the_identification_of_subgroups_with_certain_combinations_of_co_occurring_ecg_statements_pathologies": "The identification of subgroups with certain combinations of co-occurring ECG statements/pathologies involves categorizing patients based on specific patterns or combinations of ECG findings. This approach aims to reveal distinct patient subgroups that may have unique clinical implications or require tailored diagnostic and therapeutic strategies."},
{"in_depth_study__is_a_promising_direction_for__future_research": "Is a promising direction for indicates that the in-depth study is considered to have potential for yielding significant and beneficial results in the specified area of future research."},
{"future_research": "Future research denotes the ongoing and upcoming investigative efforts aimed at expanding knowledge and understanding in a given field, in this instance, the potential clinical implications of ECG signal analysis."},
{"in_depth_study__can_potentially_provide__pointers_for_future_clinical_investigations": "The phrase \"can potentially provide\" indicates a possibility or likelihood that the in-depth study might offer valuable insights or information. It suggests that the study has the capacity to yield beneficial outcomes, though these are not guaranteed."},
{"pointers_for_future_clinical_investigations": "Pointers for future clinical investigations refer to clues, suggestions, or directions that can guide further research in the clinical field. These pointers can help researchers identify areas that require more attention, propose new hypotheses, or develop novel methodologies for clinical studies."},
{"ptb_xl__comes_along_with__associated_likelihoods_for_each_diagnostic_label": "The phrase \"comes along with\" indicates that PTB-XL is accompanied by or includes certain features or data as part of its offering. In this context, it signifies that PTB-XL is provided together with associated likelihoods for each diagnostic label."},
{"associated_likelihoods_for_each_diagnostic_label": "The associated likelihoods for each diagnostic label in PTB-XL are numerical values ranging from 15 to 100. These values represent the confidence level or uncertainty assessment of the annotating cardiologist regarding each diagnostic label, with 15 indicating less confidence and 100 indicating strong confidence."},
{"likelihoods": "Likelihoods in PTB-XL are numerical values assigned to each diagnostic label, reflecting the confidence level of the annotating cardiologist regarding the accuracy of the diagnosis.|Here is another description: The likelihoods refer to the associated probabilities assigned to each diagnostic label in the PTB-XL dataset, which range from 15 to 100, indicating varying levels of confidence in the diagnosis.|Here is another description: Likelihoods in PTB-XL are numerical values assigned to each diagnostic label, ranging from 15 to 100, indicating the level of confidence the annotating cardiologist has in that particular diagnosis.|Here is another description: The likelihoods refer to the probabilities or measures of certainty associated with specific outcomes or events, in this context, related to diagnostic statements."},
{"likelihoods__range_from__15_to_100": "The range from indicates the spectrum within which the likelihoods for diagnostic labels in PTB-XL vary, providing a quantitative measure of confidence from minimal to maximal."},
{"15_to_100": "The range of 15 to 100 represents the minimum and maximum values of likelihoods assigned to diagnostic labels in PTB-XL, with 15 signifying low confidence and 100 indicating high confidence."},
{"15__indicates__less_confidence_for_one_label": "The term 'indicates' serves as a relational descriptor, showing that the number 15 is associated with a certain level of confidence in the context of diagnostic labels."},
{"less_confidence_for_one_label": "'Less confidence for one label' refers to a lower level of certainty assigned to a specific diagnostic label within the PTB-XL system, as denoted by the likelihood value of 15."},
{"100": "The number 100 in this context refers to a specific value within a range of likelihoods associated with diagnostic labels in the PTB-XL dataset."},
{"100__indicates__strong_confidence_for_one_label": "The predicate 'indicates' signifies that the subject value (100) serves as an indicator or signal for a certain level of confidence in the diagnostic label."},
{"strong_confidence_for_one_label": "The object'strong confidence for one label' describes the level of certainty or assurance that the annotating cardiologist has assigned to a particular diagnostic label within the PTB-XL dataset."},
{"likelihoods__were_extracted_from__the_original_ecg_report_string": "The phrase 'were extracted from' indicates the process by which the likelihoods were obtained. It suggests that these values were derived or pulled out from a specific source based on certain criteria or keywords."},
{"the_original_ecg_report_string": "The original ECG report string is the source document from which the likelihoods were extracted. It contains diagnostic statements that were analyzed to assign likelihood values to each diagnostic label."},
{"likelihoods__based_on__certain_keywords": "The likelihoods are determined based on specific keywords found within the original ECG report strings, reflecting the cardiologist's assessment of uncertainty for each diagnostic statement."},
{"certain_keywords": "Certain keywords within the ECG report strings are used as criteria to extract and assign likelihoods to diagnostic labels, helping to quantify the cardiologist's confidence in each diagnosis."},
{"likelihood_information": "Likelihood information in PTB-XL refers to the associated probabilities assigned to each diagnostic label, which range from 15 to 100. These probabilities are derived from the original ECG report strings using specific keywords.|Here is another description: Likelihood information in PTB-XL refers to the confidence levels assigned to each diagnostic label by the annotating cardiologist, ranging from 15 to 100. These likelihoods are derived from specific keywords in the original ECG report and reflect the individual cardiologist's assessment of the certainty of a diagnosis."},
{"likelihood_information__represents__an_individual_uncertainty_assessment_of_the_annotating_cardiologist": "The term 'represents' in this context signifies that the likelihood information is a direct reflection or expression of something else. Here, it indicates that the likelihood values are a manifestation of the cardiologist's individual assessment of uncertainty regarding the diagnostic labels."},
{"an_individual_uncertainty_assessment_of_the_annotating_cardiologist": "An individual uncertainty assessment of the annotating cardiologist refers to the personal judgment and confidence level that the cardiologist has regarding the accuracy and reliability of each diagnostic label assigned to an ECG. This assessment is unique to each cardiologist and is based on their interpretation of the ECG data."},
{"likelihood_information__is_not_directly_comparable_to__uncertainty_assessments_from_inter_rater_agreement": "The phrase \"is not directly comparable to\" indicates a fundamental difference or incompatibility between two entities. In this context, it highlights that the likelihood information provided by the cardiologist cannot be directly equated or measured against another form of uncertainty assessment."},
{"uncertainty_assessments_from_inter_rater_agreement": "Uncertainty assessments from inter-rater agreement involve evaluating the consistency or agreement among multiple raters or cardiologists when diagnosing ECGs. This form of assessment provides a measure of reliability and consensus among different experts, which is distinct from the individual likelihood assessments made by a single cardiologist."},
{"inter_rater_agreement": "Inter-rater agreement refers to the degree of consistency or consensus among different raters or observers when evaluating or categorizing the same set of data or observations."},
{"inter_rater_agreement__is_not_available_for__ptb_xl": "The phrase \"is not available for\" indicates a lack of presence or provision of something specific in relation to a particular context or entity."},
{"initial_experiment": "The initial experiment refers to the first set of trials conducted to evaluate a specific aspect or hypothesis. In this context, it involves comparing likelihoods to model uncertainty estimates for a model trained on diagnostic statements."},
{"initial_experiment__assess__quality_of_likelihood_information": "The predicate \"assess\" in this context means to evaluate or measure the effectiveness, accuracy, or quality of something. Here, it involves examining how well the likelihood information aligns with model uncertainty estimates."},
{"quality_of_likelihood_information": "The quality of likelihood information refers to how well the likelihood values produced by a model correspond to the actual uncertainties or probabilities of the outcomes. In this experiment, it is being evaluated by comparing these likelihoods to model uncertainty estimates derived from an ensemble of models."},
{"likelihoods__compare_to__model_uncertainty_estimates": "The comparison of likelihoods to model uncertainty estimates involves evaluating how well the likelihoods align with the uncertainties predicted by a model, providing insights into the model's performance and reliability."},
{"model_uncertainty_estimates": "Model uncertainty estimates are values that quantify the uncertainty or confidence level of a model's predictions, derived from the variance of an ensemble of models with different random initializations in this case."},
{"model__trained_on__diagnostic_statements": "The predicate \"trained on\" indicates the process by which the model was developed and optimized. It signifies that the model's learning and parameter adjustments were based on a specific dataset, in this case, diagnostic statements."},
{"model_uncertainty__quantified_by__variance_of_an_ensemble_of_identical_models": "Quantified by indicates the method or metric used to measure or express the subject. In this case, model uncertainty is measured using the variance of an ensemble of identical models, which provides a numerical value representing the level of uncertainty."},
{"variance_of_an_ensemble_of_identical_models": "The variance of an ensemble of identical models represents the spread or dispersion of predictions made by multiple models that are structurally identical but initialized with different random parameters. This metric is used to quantify model uncertainty, reflecting how much the predictions vary across the ensemble."},
{"ensemble__consists_of__10_models": "The predicate \"consists of\" indicates the composition of the ensemble, specifying the number of models that make up the ensemble. It denotes the relationship between the ensemble and its constituent models."},
